WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:01.890
- You know, after what
we've seen in the last year,

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:05.059
that new rail is really,
really significantly needed.

00:00:05.059 --> 00:00:07.630
As much as Rumble is
needed as a video platform,

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:10.040
I actually believe that the actual rail,

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.780
the cloud, the infrastructure,
the service is really,

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.400
really needed for the entire internet.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.040
The free and open
internet is under attack.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.010
And we need to provide the
rail as a common carrier,

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.136
as close to a common carrier as possible

00:00:25.136 --> 00:00:27.745
for everyone else out there,
all the other businesses,

00:00:27.745 --> 00:00:31.658
all the other, churches,
everything, universities,

00:00:31.658 --> 00:00:34.830
it kind of spreads into
every sector out there.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.817
And we really wanna provide
that infrastructure.

00:00:37.817 --> 00:00:40.484
(upbeat music)

00:00:44.626 --> 00:00:46.050
- I'm Dave Rubin and joining me today

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:50.420
is the CEO of Rumble, Chris Pavlovski,

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:52.460
welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:56.480
- Dave, thanks for having me
really excited to be on today.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.270
- All right, I guess we have
to do like the disclaimers

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:01.760
or whatever you wanna
call it right up front.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.760
Yes, everybody knows that
Rumble and Locals have merged.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:08.670
There was an acquisition.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.740
So this is not going to be an
attack you kind of interview.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:15.340
But the reason we wanted
to do this was to clarify

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:17.776
some of the reasons that
we're doing this partnership

00:01:17.776 --> 00:01:18.930
in the first place.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.700
So I'm just putting that part out there

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.500
before I do anything else.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:24.970
Can you just tell me a little bit

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:27.540
about how and why you created Rumble?

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.560
Because this is not
something that just magically

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.990
appeared out of nowhere a year ago.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:34.370
You've been working on
this thing for a long time.

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:35.910
- No, I have for sure.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:39.732
So, I've actually been in this
space for two decades now.

00:01:39.732 --> 00:01:43.820
I started, it was like 2001.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:45.310
So yeah, it's exactly two decades.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:47.380
And I remember it was about 2005.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.810
I had one of my friends
send me an ICQ message

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.930
or an MSN messenger message.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.220
And they're like, hey,
check this site out.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:55.390
And it was YouTube.

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:58.370
And at the time my friend
owned like the top 50 website

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.578
on the internet in video.

00:02:00.578 --> 00:02:03.278
And he's like, these
guys are gonna crush me.

00:02:03.278 --> 00:02:06.430
They're well-funded, I got nothing.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.350
I won't be able to keep
up with the funding

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:09.330
and they're gonna win.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.800
And fast forward two years,

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.030
YouTube sucks up all
the oxygen in the room.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.096
Google buys them, Google puts
them into the search engine

00:02:17.096 --> 00:02:21.540
and they end up being the
de facto video platform

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:25.400
on the internet post 2007, 2008,

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.430
and kind of monopolize the entire,

00:02:27.430 --> 00:02:31.857
not kinda, they monopolize the
entire video sphere online.

00:02:31.857 --> 00:02:34.310
So it puts all of us out of business

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.210
that own any video sites or small sites

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.150
where no longer existing.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:41.440
And then it wasn't until about 2010,

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.970
where I started seeing a shift change,

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:49.970
a real change in the way
YouTube was doing things,

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.160
they introduced multi-channel networks,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.520
they introduced a lot of preferencing

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.746
on big corporations and icons.

00:02:57.746 --> 00:03:02.040
So it was about, it took me
till about 2013 where I said,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.200
it's time to get back into this space.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.350
It really seems to me that
the incumbent platform,

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:10.930
YouTube at the time is starting

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:13.234
to really deprioritize the small creator

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:16.100
and really prioritize those
multi-channel networks,

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:20.175
big influencers, large
brands, large corporations,

00:03:20.175 --> 00:03:24.160
and our aunts and our uncles
and our friends and family

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.946
no longer really had any
kind of space or real estate

00:03:26.946 --> 00:03:29.840
on these platforms anymore.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.665
So we started Rumble in
2013 to really bring back

00:03:32.665 --> 00:03:36.200
that fairness and really
help that small creator

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:40.430
get access to those tools, get
access to the monetization,

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.250
get access to everything that those MCNs

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:44.165
were giving creators access to.

00:03:44.165 --> 00:03:48.240
And we built a pretty
good business in 2013,

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.130
and it was a good business
all the way up until 2020.

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:54.716
And then it became a really
good business in 2000,

00:03:54.716 --> 00:03:57.360
I would say, starting in 2018 and forward

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.598
it became a really, really good business.

00:03:59.598 --> 00:04:03.030
By 2020, everything hits the fan.

00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:07.390
It was like 11 o'clock at night
in the middle of the summer.

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:10.400
And I remember a Congressman
signing up to our site.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:11.950
And I was like, holy shit.

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:15.520
Like why is a Congressman
signing up to Rumble?

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:19.300
Like this being a Canadian,
a Canadian video platform.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.640
That was a pretty big deal.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.787
And I remember going to
my colleague and saying,

00:04:24.787 --> 00:04:26.510
"Hey, is this real?

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:27.830
Is this a real signup?"

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:30.400
And we looked at it and
we think it was real.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.547
It seemed like it was real.

00:04:32.547 --> 00:04:33.380
And a week later,

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:34.610
I'm on the phone with this Congressman

00:04:34.610 --> 00:04:37.066
and he asks me a simple, simple question.

00:04:37.066 --> 00:04:39.430
If I put my content on Rumble,

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:42.690
will I be able to find it in
search if I search my name?

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:46.530
And when we're like, yeah,
that seems pretty standard.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:49.080
It should come up in our search
if someone searches for you.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.810
And he's like, "Well, that's
not happening anywhere

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:51.670
else on the internet.

00:04:51.670 --> 00:04:53.170
It's not happening on YouTube.

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:56.150
So I am gonna bring it over to Rumble."

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:00.260
Within three months, less
than that, two months,

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:03.437
he accumulates over 300,000
subscribers on Rumble

00:05:03.437 --> 00:05:05.644
and in the very same period,

00:05:05.644 --> 00:05:07.960
over five, actually a longer period,

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:09.760
five years on being on YouTube,

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:12.230
he only had 10,000 subscribers.

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:15.800
So you can kind of see how that happened.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.100
And when that did happen
and everybody noticed,

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.350
everything hit the fan.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:22.300
Everyone started hearing about Rumble.

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:24.780
People were like growing faster on Rumble.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:25.660
The message went out.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:26.950
This Congressman was on TV,

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:28.683
telling everybody to go to Rumble.

00:05:30.256 --> 00:05:31.390
- I know who the answer is

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:34.370
but we should tell people
who the Congressman was.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:35.925
- It was Congressman Devin Nunes.

00:05:35.925 --> 00:05:36.758
- Yeah.

00:05:36.758 --> 00:05:41.290
- He was the very first person
and the political sphere

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:44.380
to come on onto Rumble
and realized this problem

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:45.240
that was happening.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.214
And when it did happen, he was out on TV,

00:05:48.214 --> 00:05:49.660
letting everybody know that,

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:51.630
hey, like I'm growing faster on Rumble

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.390
and I'm getting more views on Rumble

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:55.020
than I am getting on YouTube.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.856
And it wasn't long after
that till started following

00:05:57.856 --> 00:06:00.150
and jumping onto Rumble.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:03.600
And it took us to we're in 2021 now.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:06.950
And in August we saw the
largest month we've ever seen

00:06:06.950 --> 00:06:09.270
on our platform, a
tremendous amount of growth

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:12.615
from where we were six months
ago, seven, eight months ago.

00:06:12.615 --> 00:06:16.194
The largest single growth in
a single month was in August.

00:06:16.194 --> 00:06:18.910
And we haven't looked back since,

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:21.200
and it's been super exciting,

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:25.600
we did the merger and I
wanna call it a merger,

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.570
well it's not really an acquisition.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:31.000
And I think we should really
explore that a little bit more,

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.300
but the merger with you

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:34.860
and becoming partners with you and Assaf

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.209
is the next major moment in this business.

00:06:38.209 --> 00:06:42.877
And I'm super excited to be here with you

00:06:42.877 --> 00:06:43.980
and talk through this.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.070
- So before we get into that stuff.

00:06:46.070 --> 00:06:47.860
Can you just tell people a little bit

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:50.350
about what you actually have built?

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:51.870
Like when people think, oh,

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:53.750
you're trying to compete with YouTube

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.700
or you're trying to
compete with Amazon AWS,

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:59.020
like these sound like impossible feats

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:00.730
and you know as well as I, like,

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:02.490
I went into enough
boardrooms to tell people

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:04.070
what we were doing with
Locals and they're just like,

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:05.251
no, the big boys exist.

00:07:05.251 --> 00:07:07.243
Like accept it and move on.

00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:11.340
- Yeah and this is really like looking

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:12.569
under the hood and it's really important.

00:07:12.569 --> 00:07:13.402
It's a great question.

00:07:13.402 --> 00:07:16.720
So one of the things we did is
we did, when we built Rumble,

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:18.091
we built it the old
fashioned way, like I said,

00:07:18.091 --> 00:07:19.900
I've been in this for two decades.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:22.420
I've been working off bare
metal and my own servers

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:27.103
since the day I started in
2001 or two or whenever it was.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:29.740
And I never really did it any other way.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:33.377
All these fancy words,
cloud and AWS and Amazon,

00:07:33.377 --> 00:07:35.540
I never subscribed to that.

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:38.120
And when we started Rumble,
we put in our own servers,

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:40.340
we put in, use our own
bare metal and we built out

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:42.030
our own infrastructure from the ground up.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:44.560
And we didn't look back
in how we did that.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:49.470
And it wasn't something that
I thought was necessarily,

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.140
it wasn't like, hey, we need to do this

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:52.560
because we need to compete against them.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:53.900
It was like, hey, we need to do this

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.370
because it's gonna save us costs.

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:56.203
It's better.

00:07:56.203 --> 00:07:57.800
It's just, we own everything.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:00.704
It's, we don't wanna give
anybody access to that data.

00:08:00.704 --> 00:08:03.280
We wanna make sure
that's all under our hood

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.230
and not under someone else's hood.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:06.590
And that's kind of why we did it.

00:08:06.590 --> 00:08:10.703
And you come to 2020 and
2019, 2020, and 2021.

00:08:10.703 --> 00:08:14.580
It ends up being a real,
real good decision.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:16.840
But that's not to say that we didn't look

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:18.320
into the future a little bit with it.

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:21.750
When we started Rumble, we
saw Rumble as like two things.

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:24.890
And one was to compete
aggressively against YouTube

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:27.570
and the video platform
as much as possible.

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:30.370
And, but if we successfully do that,

00:08:30.370 --> 00:08:34.700
it means that we'll now have
the scale to go really compete

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:37.370
against all these
incumbent cloud services.

00:08:37.370 --> 00:08:42.370
Remember when you look at
Google and Google cloud,

00:08:43.170 --> 00:08:45.860
what do they have that makes
their infrastructure so large?

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:46.700
YouTube.

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:48.500
When you look at Amazon, AWS,

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:49.660
what do they have that makes

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:51.530
their infrastructure really large?

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:53.950
It's amazon.com and Twitch.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:57.290
When you look at Microsoft
Azure, you look at X-Box,

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:59.130
you look at these things
that really allow them

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.270
to have real competitive advantage

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:02.550
on the infrastructure side.

00:09:02.550 --> 00:09:04.580
When you look at Rumble
and now that Rumble

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:08.500
has become a formidable size, we now have

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:12.520
that advantage to really
offer a cloud services,

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:13.790
really offer a new rail.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:16.310
And after what we've
seen in the last year,

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:19.870
that new rail is really,
really significantly needed.

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:22.060
As much as Rumble is
needed as a video platform.

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:25.190
I actually believe that
the actual rail, the cloud,

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:27.430
the infrastructure, the service

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:31.620
is really, really needed
for the entire internet.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:34.450
The free and open
internet is under attack.

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:37.440
And we need to provide the
rail as a common carrier,

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.170
as close to a common carrier as possible

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:42.830
for everyone else out there,
all the other businesses,

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:46.170
all the other churches,
everything, universities,

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:49.240
it kind of spreads into
every sector out there.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:51.790
And we really wanna provide
that infrastructure.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:53.740
- So I'm sure many people
are watching this going.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:55.100
So let me get this straight.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:56.740
If Parlor let's say,

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.480
had been on Rumble infrastructure

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:02.810
using Rumble infrastructure
after January 6th,

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.010
would Parlor still be here right now?

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.933
- Absolutely, Rumble is still around.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:09.780
- Right.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:12.790
- I think that's a
testament to where we are

00:10:12.790 --> 00:10:14.645
and what we're doing.

00:10:14.645 --> 00:10:18.860
Rumble is around, and
Rumble will be around.

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:20.776
And we're gonna continue
to build that rail

00:10:20.776 --> 00:10:24.035
and build that new economy for everyone,

00:10:24.035 --> 00:10:27.670
not just for rumble.com and
Locals, but for everyone.

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:28.840
- And let me get this straight Chris,

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:32.240
our policy is that people can
basically say what they want,

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.760
and we're not gonna decide
who the winners or losers are.

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:37.220
And if maybe somebody broke
the laws of the United States,

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:39.760
they have a bigger problem
than Chris and Dave is that,

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.823
could we be doing something
so radical like that?

00:10:41.823 --> 00:10:46.660
- Yeah, I think that the
best way to look at it

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:48.760
is that we've got to get to becoming,

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:51.370
it is close to a common
carrier as possible.

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:52.280
We're not a publisher.

00:10:52.280 --> 00:10:54.070
We don't wanna get an editorial control.

00:10:54.070 --> 00:10:56.550
We wanna tell people what
they can or can't say,

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:58.120
if you're having an argument
at the dinner table,

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:00.200
why can't you have that
argument when you're online?

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.410
If you're having a discussion
or having an opinion,

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:03.970
why can't that be online?

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:05.320
This is the free and open internet,

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:06.690
is as free as your dinner table.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:09.450
There no difference in my
mind and there shouldn't be.

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:11.410
And what we've seen happen
in the last 10 years

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:13.940
on the internet is this huge tilting

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:16.541
to kind of shut everybody up regardless

00:11:16.541 --> 00:11:18.370
of your political views.

00:11:18.370 --> 00:11:20.760
They just, every, they wanna shut down

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.580
the conversation that we all
have at our dinner tables.

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:26.220
And that's just, it boggles my mind

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:28.880
because when you look
back 10, 15 years ago,

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:30.610
and you look at these incumbent platforms,

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:32.870
they were all preaching
the free and open internet.

00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:36.830
I even think I saw Twitter,
Twitter verified or Twitter.

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:38.920
I'm not sure it's Twitter policy say

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:41.630
that they want a free and
open internet in Nigeria.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:44.640
And like, where is that
in the rest of the world?

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:46.820
Like, there's still pretending

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:50.060
they want a free and open
internet, but it really is not.

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.802
And I think that, us
building the rails to that

00:11:52.802 --> 00:11:56.610
and really providing an
open communication platform

00:11:56.610 --> 00:11:59.730
like Rumble are small pieces,

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:02.010
but important pieces to making sure

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:04.120
the internet stays free and stays open.

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:07.554
- So I wanna flash back
to all the way in June

00:12:07.554 --> 00:12:12.554
of this past year, June, 2021,
Assaf my partner in Locals,

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:14.400
and I, we met here in Miami.

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:16.210
We had a couple of plans,
we thought, all right,

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:19.150
we're gonna present this guy
with some partnership ideas.

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:20.553
We can work together in some ways.

00:12:20.553 --> 00:12:22.900
And I've said this a
couple of times publicly,

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:27.300
but literally before the water
was even poured at the table,

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.320
we had sat down for about 20 seconds.

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:31.100
You said, guys, I wanna merge with you.

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:33.150
How do we make this thing happen?

00:12:33.150 --> 00:12:36.380
So why were you interested in Locals,

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:38.493
created by this wacky YouTuber?

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.331
- Well, that's a pretty easy one.

00:12:45.331 --> 00:12:49.430
You build something that
is really important.

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:53.380
And what you've built is
you've allowed creators

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:57.410
to basically build
subscriptions in their own way,

00:12:57.410 --> 00:13:01.400
allowing creators to have
autonomy and independence

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:03.180
in a different stream of revenue.

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:05.840
Rumble has been primarily
since we started,

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:07.592
it's been based off of
advertising and advertising is

00:13:07.592 --> 00:13:11.690
to be frank, it can be a
weakness in this environment,

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:14.193
and we don't wanna fall pressure
to that type of weakness.

00:13:14.193 --> 00:13:17.360
So by having like having Locals

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:18.810
and having a revenue stream,

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:22.160
that's protected by subscriptions
and allowing creators

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:26.450
to have that independence is
something that I've admired.

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:28.240
And I've always wanted to get towards,

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:30.800
we've built Rumble Rants,
we've built in tipping.

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.190
But then I said to myself, like,

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:34.070
why would we compete against someone

00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:35.800
that has the same philosophy as us

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:37.210
that thinks the same way as us?

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:38.690
And we're up against the largest thing,

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:40.670
the largest companies on the planet,

00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:42.358
we're up against the monopoly.

00:13:42.358 --> 00:13:45.290
It makes no sense to be doing it,

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:49.680
to be doing it with less
resources and being really small.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:53.520
You can't fight like
trillion dollar entities

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:55.010
with a couple million dollars.

00:13:55.010 --> 00:13:56.290
Like you're not gonna win.

00:13:56.290 --> 00:13:57.590
It's just, it's impossible.

00:13:57.590 --> 00:14:00.600
You need to really kind
of bring in all the minds,

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:03.230
bring in all the resources and really,

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:05.318
and really do it together.

00:14:05.318 --> 00:14:08.640
And I felt like one of the
things that seems to happen a lot

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:11.520
is that everyone wants to
kind of build their own thing

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:13.050
and kind of go at it their own way

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:16.140
without really kind of doing
it together, collectively

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:17.810
in a unified way.

00:14:17.810 --> 00:14:21.570
And I felt like, we've
been talking to Assaf

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:23.210
for quite some time.

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:25.720
I felt like there was a real
opportunity after meeting you

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:29.850
guys in person and then
doing it many other times,

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:30.870
following that meeting,

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:33.060
it just made more sense to unify

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:34.950
and to do this as a collective resource

00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:38.370
and to put all our brains
together and to do it together.

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:42.320
And that's what this is,
this this isn't just me.

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:43.830
This isn't just you anymore.

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:45.950
It's not just, it's all of us.

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.710
And it's all of us gonna go up against

00:14:48.710 --> 00:14:51.450
the monopoly and that's gonna take,

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:53.810
it's gonna take more than
just us at the end of the day,

00:14:53.810 --> 00:14:56.530
we're gonna need more than
just a spotlight at this point.

00:14:56.530 --> 00:14:58.870
I think this was the perfect merge.

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:01.340
It allows Rumble to be
a lot more defensible.

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:03.630
We now have a subscription revenue stream,

00:15:03.630 --> 00:15:05.870
and it allows us to be a lot more bold

00:15:05.870 --> 00:15:07.480
on the advertising front.

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:09.466
The things that we have planned.

00:15:09.466 --> 00:15:13.140
And we've discussed on the
advertising stuff is really bold.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:14.516
And I can't wait till

00:15:14.516 --> 00:15:16.770
we go to market with those strategies,

00:15:16.770 --> 00:15:19.120
but now that we have
Locals and we're together,

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:22.170
we can now do that and we can
do things and we can innovate

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:24.720
in the market the way we
couldn't innovate before.

00:15:24.720 --> 00:15:29.517
So they, I think for me, it
was a no brainer to combine

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:32.960
and work with the two,
your yourself and Assaf,

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:36.750
I think, this is the perfect synergy.

00:15:36.750 --> 00:15:37.813
- And for us,

00:15:37.813 --> 00:15:40.550
it gave us not only the
infrastructure we needed,

00:15:40.550 --> 00:15:43.341
but resources, but I
think you really hit it,

00:15:43.341 --> 00:15:44.520
which is that we all vibe,

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:46.530
like we all know what the threat is.

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:48.410
We all want to fight it.

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:50.340
And you said collective a couple of times,

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:51.173
and it's like,

00:15:51.173 --> 00:15:52.610
my audience freaks out
at the word collective,

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:54.890
but like sometimes you've
got to team with people

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:56.180
to beat these things.

00:15:56.180 --> 00:15:59.430
So let's just get into some
of the specifics of the deal

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:00.440
and how we're gonna work together.

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:02.550
Because a lot of people
in my local's community

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:04.690
and on YouTube and on Rumble were asking

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:06.060
about some of the specifics.

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:10.550
So first off, Locals is gonna
continue to operate as is,

00:16:10.550 --> 00:16:13.200
we just have better resources behind us,

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:14.530
Assaf will continue to run it.

00:16:14.530 --> 00:16:17.110
You're not looking to take over Locals.

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:19.190
Anything else you want to say on that?

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:21.850
- Yeah, like the, our vision,

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:24.918
and it was from day one
is that we'll use Rumble

00:16:24.918 --> 00:16:28.810
to funnel a massive audience
to Locals for your creators,

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:31.800
bring them more subscribers
integrate it into Rumble,

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:35.380
allow the Rumble audience
to really find Locals,

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:37.780
creators and subscribe to their pages.

00:16:37.780 --> 00:16:39.970
A real true discovery for Locals

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:43.210
which drives the bottom
line for all the creators.

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:44.130
In addition to that,

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:46.120
the other thing that we
thought was really compelling

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:49.950
was in necessary and absolutely necessary

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:52.060
is to get Locals on the Rumble rail,

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:56.180
is to utilize that infrastructure
so that you're protected.

00:16:56.180 --> 00:16:58.420
And that you can continue
to fulfill your promise

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:00.030
of allowing freedom of expression

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:04.390
and allowing people to
have their own opinions

00:17:04.390 --> 00:17:06.710
and have their discussions
the way they want to have

00:17:06.710 --> 00:17:08.510
without anybody impeding that.

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:12.920
And my philosophy is that the way Locals

00:17:12.920 --> 00:17:16.030
is being run right now,
and it has been phenomenal.

00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:18.500
There's no reason for anybody
to step in front of that.

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:21.180
And that's not what this is, what this is,

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:23.490
is that Locals is gonna
be running independently.

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:25.330
Rumble is gonna be running independently,

00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:27.810
and we're gonna utilize those resources,

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:29.600
bring in that distribution from Rumble,

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:31.865
really help that Locals
community, and really

00:17:31.865 --> 00:17:34.330
protect that Locals community
on an infrastructure

00:17:34.330 --> 00:17:36.110
point of view, nothing else changes.

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:37.350
Why would anything else changes?

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:39.470
That's not gonna help anybody.

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:42.760
- Can you talk about the data
and content side of this also,

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:44.330
because when I started this thing,

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:46.681
it was purely because I needed a place

00:17:46.681 --> 00:17:50.670
that I would own my content,
my video, my audio, et cetera.

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:51.580
And I needed a place

00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:53.510
where if I got booted off everything else,

00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:56.400
I could actually still
communicate with my audience.

00:17:56.400 --> 00:17:58.930
That's exactly how we built Locals,

00:17:58.930 --> 00:18:00.840
but I saw some people
there saying, oh, oh,

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:03.210
now Rumble is gonna have all of that data.

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:05.160
You won't have it as the creator,

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:06.850
but I don't wanna give you too much

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:07.683
of a leading question here.

00:18:07.683 --> 00:18:09.110
That's actually not the case.

00:18:10.740 --> 00:18:12.990
- It's funny, 'cause
I was actually talking

00:18:12.990 --> 00:18:14.521
about this like a year
ago and I've been having

00:18:14.521 --> 00:18:19.190
this conversation for
months since that first time

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:20.060
that I explored this.

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:23.650
I've always wanted every creator on Rumble

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:24.910
to own their data.

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:25.790
I think that's imperative.

00:18:25.790 --> 00:18:28.190
I think I wrote an article
a long time ago about that.

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:29.970
And that was one of the key ways

00:18:29.970 --> 00:18:32.040
to kind of really kind of
get back into this market

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:34.810
is really making sure that
the creators are independent

00:18:34.810 --> 00:18:35.930
and own that data.

00:18:35.930 --> 00:18:38.090
For us to like have that data on Rumble,

00:18:38.090 --> 00:18:40.260
that's not the mission here.

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:42.810
The mission is to make sure
that creators stay independent.

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:45.050
Their mission is to
protect the creator economy

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:46.980
more than ever and protect their opinions

00:18:46.980 --> 00:18:48.540
and protect what they wanna do.

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:50.630
It would be, it would make no sense

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:53.530
to be doing the complete
opposite of why these businesses

00:18:53.530 --> 00:18:55.190
are succeeding today.

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:58.019
Like we are here to like
change the creator economy,

00:18:58.019 --> 00:19:01.100
give them more autonomy,
give them more independence.

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:04.070
I really think this is the next wave

00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:06.400
of web 3.0, call it whatever you want,

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:07.770
but it's surely the next wave.

00:19:07.770 --> 00:19:09.580
And I think Locals is
on the forefront of that

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:12.337
and Rumble needs to catch
up and do more of that.

00:19:12.337 --> 00:19:14.180
And that's what Locals allows us to do

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:15.954
is like now we can accelerate on that.

00:19:15.954 --> 00:19:18.904
Imagine having all your
subscribers on Rumble,

00:19:18.904 --> 00:19:22.600
being portable, that should be possible.

00:19:22.600 --> 00:19:24.050
That's what we should stand for.

00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:26.280
That's what the creator economy deserves.

00:19:26.280 --> 00:19:27.720
Those are, that's their audience.

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:30.900
For us to be able to take that
away from them is not right.

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:32.870
And that's it and that's
what Locals is doing.

00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:34.532
They've changed that if anything,

00:19:34.532 --> 00:19:36.433
Locals is gonna get Rumble
to do the same thing

00:19:36.433 --> 00:19:38.840
that Locals is doing, not the vice-versa.

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:41.100
- And to be clear, that's complete 180,

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:44.080
the reverse of what happens
on Twitter, YouTube elsewhere.

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:48.410
Like I have no way of contacting
my 1.7 or whatever it is,

00:19:48.410 --> 00:19:50.010
million YouTube subscribers,

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:51.900
because they own the
access to those people.

00:19:51.900 --> 00:19:53.990
I don't have direct
communication with them.

00:19:53.990 --> 00:19:56.160
One of the things that I
saw pop up a couple of times

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:58.640
when we announced this
was people were concerned

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:01.110
that Rumble is a Canadian company.

00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:04.280
You are a scary Canadian with
that draconian government

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:05.280
you have over there.

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:07.730
Do we have any plans on that front?

00:20:07.730 --> 00:20:10.240
- So we did announce
that we were opening up

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:14.210
a US headquarters in Longboat
Key in Sarasota County

00:20:14.210 --> 00:20:15.043
down in Florida.

00:20:15.043 --> 00:20:18.210
So we'll be neighbors to Locals.

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:22.350
I know Locals is in Miami and
we'll be on the other side

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:25.755
of the coast, but yeah,
we're definitely having,

00:20:25.755 --> 00:20:29.210
we're planting a large
presence in the United States

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:31.300
and we're super excited about that.

00:20:31.300 --> 00:20:36.300
That's the business climate
in Florida is amazing.

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:38.010
The county has been amazing.

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:41.048
And one of the things I
like about it is that,

00:20:41.048 --> 00:20:45.120
Rumble gets a lot of
support from small towns

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:48.080
in the United States and
big cities, but small towns,

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:50.340
a lot of it and going into a small town

00:20:50.340 --> 00:20:52.280
is super exciting to me.

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:54.670
I think supporting a community like that

00:20:54.670 --> 00:20:57.790
and really bringing Rumble
to a community like that

00:20:57.790 --> 00:21:01.703
rather than picking a large
city is a super exciting,

00:21:01.703 --> 00:21:03.280
I'm looking forward to it.

00:21:03.280 --> 00:21:05.430
But yeah, I understand
everybody's concerns

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:09.963
about being in Canada, those are concerns.

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:12.810
I don't, we don't know
where that's gonna go

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:15.378
here in Canada yet, the
laws are still kind of,

00:21:15.378 --> 00:21:20.070
they haven't been passed yet,
but it's definitely a concern.

00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:22.680
I think a lot of companies
are expressing that concern.

00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:24.718
And at this point right now,

00:21:24.718 --> 00:21:27.300
like we'll have our US
headquarters in Sarasota,

00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:28.900
and I don't think there's anything

00:21:28.900 --> 00:21:30.570
for anybody to worry about at this point.

00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:32.060
- Is there anything else on the tech side

00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:33.880
that we should let people know

00:21:33.880 --> 00:21:35.800
that maybe I haven't let them know

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:37.160
or Bongino hasn't let them know

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:39.050
or that you just want people to know about

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:40.600
what it is that we're building?

00:21:42.340 --> 00:21:46.390
- So there's, the Rumble
cloud is something

00:21:46.390 --> 00:21:48.410
that we're gonna put a lot of focus on.

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:50.740
Obviously, our first priority right now

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:52.950
is to make sure that we
get Locals onto this cloud.

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:55.949
We've brought on a lot of
customers on the cloud side

00:21:55.949 --> 00:21:58.030
and it's funny

00:21:58.030 --> 00:22:00.260
'cause we're not even really
proactively selling it.

00:22:00.260 --> 00:22:02.000
We've made some statements that we have it

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:04.560
and we've had a lot of
incoming and we've onboarded

00:22:04.560 --> 00:22:08.490
a lot of different clients,
some very large ones as well.

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:12.710
So there's gonna be a lot
of stuff on the cloud front.

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:14.420
We're gonna be launching that Rumble cloud

00:22:14.420 --> 00:22:16.650
in the near future.

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:18.550
But in terms of Locals and Rumble,

00:22:18.550 --> 00:22:22.800
there's gonna be a lot of tech
integration there as well,

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:25.054
not just on the cloud side,
but on the front side,

00:22:25.054 --> 00:22:28.680
being able to access to
Locals content through Rumble,

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:30.940
being able to discover
it, having your accounts,

00:22:30.940 --> 00:22:33.880
to be able to connect,
not on a data perspective.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:35.670
So we're not taking the data,

00:22:35.670 --> 00:22:38.410
but on a perspective of
like being able to say,

00:22:38.410 --> 00:22:40.200
hey, you have a Locals account

00:22:40.200 --> 00:22:43.260
and advertise that Locals
account on your Rumble page.

00:22:43.260 --> 00:22:46.370
So you can go there and convert
and maybe even convert them

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:49.330
straight from the Rumble page
into the Locals community.

00:22:49.330 --> 00:22:51.142
So there's a lot of work on that front.

00:22:51.142 --> 00:22:54.780
We also have our new app coming out.

00:22:54.780 --> 00:22:58.790
That'll be out in Q1 of
2022, hopefully sooner,

00:22:58.790 --> 00:23:01.170
but let's say Q1 2022,

00:23:01.170 --> 00:23:03.524
which is gonna have a
completely new interface.

00:23:03.524 --> 00:23:05.890
I know that's been a huge complaint.

00:23:05.890 --> 00:23:08.440
And I agree, when you're
a bootstrap company

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:13.440
and you never raised money
since like five, six months ago,

00:23:13.540 --> 00:23:16.980
you kinda, you don't really
have the cash resources

00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:19.500
to go out there and build
that fancy user interface.

00:23:19.500 --> 00:23:21.830
You kind of focus on
the core infrastructure

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:22.883
which we have done.

00:23:23.790 --> 00:23:26.090
But now that we were a
little more capitalized,

00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:28.710
we can build that interface
that everyone's looking for.

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:30.770
And we have been, and
we're more working on that

00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:31.663
